{"problem": "pluck_at\n\nplucks an element or several elements from each item in a sequence.\n\n``pluck_at`` maps ``itertoolz.get`` over a sequence and returns one or more\nelements of each item in the sequence.\n\nThis is equivalent to running `map(curried.get(ind), seqs)`\n\n``ind`` can be either a single string/index or a list of strings/indices.\n``seqs`` should be sequence containing sequences or dicts.\n\ne.g.\n\n>>> data = [{'id': 1, 'name': 'Cheese'}, {'id': 2, 'name': 'Pies'}]\n>>> list(pluck_at('name', data))\n['Cheese', 'Pies']\n>>> list(pluck_at([0, 1], [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 7]]))\n[(1, 2), (4, 5)]\n\nSee Also:\n    get\n    map", "resolution": "def pluck_at(ind, seqs, default=no_default):\n    \"\"\" plucks an element or several elements from each item in a sequence.\n\n    ``pluck_at`` maps ``itertoolz.get`` over a sequence and returns one or more\n    elements of each item in the sequence.\n\n    This is equivalent to running `map(curried.get(ind), seqs)`\n\n    ``ind`` can be either a single string/index or a list of strings/indices.\n    ``seqs`` should be sequence containing sequences or dicts.\n\n    e.g.\n\n    >>> data = [{'id': 1, 'name': 'Cheese'}, {'id': 2, 'name': 'Pies'}]\n    >>> list(pluck_at('name', data))\n    ['Cheese', 'Pies']\n    >>> list(pluck_at([0, 1], [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 7]]))\n    [(1, 2), (4, 5)]\n\n    See Also:\n        get\n        map\n    \"\"\"\n    if default == no_default:\n        get = getter(ind)\n        return map(get, seqs)\n    elif isinstance(ind, list):\n        return (tuple(_get(item, seq, default) for item in ind)\n                for seq in seqs)\n    return (_get(ind, seq, default) for seq in seqs)\n", "text": "pluck_at"}